  "image_name": "2c26e57072b6d4cba51c97e89ea0fa98.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a coordinated fasting or energy-deficit response linking the brain with peripheral organs. A central brain node integrates hormonal and metabolic signals that indicate low fuel availability. Reduced insulin from the pancreas, reduced leptin from adipose tissue, and reduced glucose from the liver all feed into the brain as inhibitory or low-energy cues. In response, the brain shifts physiology away from energy-expensive functions: reproduction and thermogenesis decrease, while feeding increases.\n\nAt the same time, the brain appears to engage major endocrine stress pathways. One branch suppresses the HPT axis, consistent with lowered heat production and metabolic rate. Another activates the HPA axis, increasing corticosterone. The figure also shows increased glucagon, and both glucagon and corticosterone act on the liver to raise hepatic glucose production (labeled as increased HGP). This helps maintain circulating fuel during scarcity.\n\nOverall, the diagram depicts an adaptive survival program triggered by deficiency of available or stored fuel. The brain senses reduced peripheral energy signals and orchestrates behavioral, endocrine, and metabolic changes that conserve energy, promote food seeking, and mobilize glucose from the liver.",
